1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:08,559
Before looking at answers, we write down what
good enough means. This matters: if we

2
00:00:08,559 --> 00:00:17,098
invent the rules after seeing a favorite, the
analysis becomes a story we tell ourselves.

3
00:00:17,098 --> 00:00:26,117
Our fictional engineer sets four rules. First,
across four practice storms, at least 6 percent

4
00:00:26,117 --> 00:00:34,752
less water must reach the model's outlet than in
the unchanged model. That unchanged model

5
00:00:34,752 --> 00:00:43,003
is our baseline. Second, the 30-year screening
cost must stay at or below 200 thousand

6
00:00:43,003 --> 00:00:50,582
dollars. Third, no storm is allowed to create a
higher peak flow at the outlet.

7
00:00:50,582 --> 00:00:59,313
A good total cannot excuse one sharper surge.
Fourth, every simulation used in the decision

8
00:00:59,313 --> 00:01:07,756
must pass basic numerical checks. The storms and
costs are invented for this lesson. And

9
00:01:07,756 --> 00:01:15,911
verified replay means the saved files still
match the recorded runs. It does not mean

10
00:01:15,911 --> 00:01:24,066
the model matches a real neighborhood. With the
question and rules fixed, we can look

11
00:01:24,066 --> 00:01:28,000
at evidence without moving the goalposts.
